## ASSEMBLED SECTION



### <img src="https://flagcdn.com/kr.svg" width="32" alt="South Korea" style={{display: 'inline', verticalAlign: 'middle', position: 'relative', top: '-2px', marginRight: '8px', border: '1px solid #d1d5db', borderRadius: '2px'}} />South Korea

South Korea's multi-vector hedging strategy demonstrates continued operational effectiveness as Lee strengthens his domestic governance position with approval rising to 63% - near his post-inauguration peak - while leveraging presidential authority to extract concrete chaebol investment commitments of 300 trillion won for regional development and youth employment. New Congressional investigation of Korean regulators introduces institutional friction within the transactional alliance framework, but Lee's aggressive anti-speculation campaign with 17-source media prominence validates his confidence in his mandate for transformative domestic policies. Economic fundamentals maintain strength despite external market volatility, with retail investors defending indices against foreign selling pressure. Lee continues operating within the constraints of multi-vector engagement established in 2025, managing alliance obligations while pursuing active economic statecraft through direct chaebol coordination.

**Key developments:**

- **Economic:** Lee hosted Samsung, SK, Hyundai, LG and other major chaebol leaders at Cheong Wa Dae, extracting commitments for 270-300 trillion won regional investments over five years and 51,600 new hires in 2026. Samsung pledged 450 trillion won domestic investment including R&D and 60,000 Korea-based hires over five years. Lee framed this as ensuring 'fruits of growth spread evenly' to regions and youth.
- **Economic:** KOSPI fell 5.26% below 5,000 threshold triggering sell-side circuit breakers, with Samsung Electronics down 6.29% and SK Hynix down 8.69%. Foreign investors recorded net sales exceeding 2.2 trillion won while individual investors defended with 4.5 trillion won net purchases. Attributed to global 'Warsh Shock' expectations of slower Fed rate cuts.
- **Domestic:** Lee intensified real estate speculation campaign through social media warnings and Cabinet orders, vowing to make holding multiple properties 'impossible' and giving multi-homeowners 'last chance' before May 10 tax break reinstatement. Rejected Finance Minister's use of 'maybe' twice, demanding certainty in policy implementation. Warned failure would impact governance 'for the remaining four years.'
- **Domestic:** National Barometer Survey shows Lee's approval rating increased 4 percentage points to 63%, highest since August 2025. Negative assessment dropped to 30%. Democratic Party support rose to 41% while People Power Party gained to 22%. On June local elections, 52% said voters should support ruling party for stability versus 36% backing opposition for checks.
- **Diplomatic:** House Judiciary Committee issued subpoena to Coupang and opened investigation into Korea Fair Trade Commission and other agencies for allegedly subjecting US firms to 'punitive obligations, excessive fines, and discriminatory enforcement practices.' Investigation aims to inform potential legislation protecting US companies from foreign discriminatory regulations.
- **Unexpected:** Bithumb cryptocurrency exchange accidentally distributed \$40 billion in bitcoin to customers.

<Accordion title="Other Stories">
- **Major South Korean crypto exchange accidentally distributes \$40 billion in bitcoin** — Bithumb, one of South Korea's largest cryptocurrency exchanges, mistakenly sent over \$40 billion worth of bitcoin to customers as promotional rewards due to employee error. The exchange recovered 99.7% of the funds within hours and restricted trading for affected customers. *([bbc.com](https://bbc.com/news/articles/cz6y62nzjxxo))*
- **President Lee faces ongoing legal proceedings on multiple fronts** — President Lee dealt with several legal issues including accusations of prosecution evidence tampering in the Wirye New Town case, criticism over prosecution appeals, and asset seizures in a development scandal case. Lee also appointed an opposition-recommended special prosecutor to investigate former President Yoon. *([chosun.com](https://chosun.com/english/national-en/2026/02/06/MACMCHHHPVBFRKRQRBJM4NT3JU))*
- **South Korean financial markets experience volatility amid global pressures** — Korean markets faced multiple pressures including KOSPI falling below 5,000 and triggering circuit breakers, Bank of Korea managing currency volatility, and concerns over Korean retail investors' record \$171 billion holdings in US stocks affecting FX stability. *([fxstreet.com](https://reuters.com/world/asia-pacific/koreas-fight-fx-stability-undermined-by-its-wall-street-mania-2026-02-04))*
- **Samsung ends software support for Galaxy S21 flagship phones from 2021** — Samsung officially discontinued software updates for its Galaxy S21 series phones launched in 2021, fulfilling its four-year update promise. The S21, S21+, and S21 Ultra will no longer receive major Android updates or security patches, though the S21 FE continues to receive support. *([notebookcheck.net](https://notebookcheck.net/Samsung-Galaxy-flagships-from-2021-will-no-longer-receive-major-software-updates.1221228.0.html))*
- **President Lee warns against stock manipulation amid reporter probe** — President Lee issued strong warnings against stock manipulation, linking his comments to an ongoing investigation into alleged advance trading by financial journalists. Lee emphasized zero tolerance for market manipulation that undermines order. *([upi.com](https://upi.com/Top_News/World-News/2026/02/06/president-lee-jae-myung-reaffirmed-zero-tolerance-stand-on-stock-manipulation/5601770428674))*
- **South Korea advances stablecoin legislation framework** — South Korea's Digital Asset Basic Act, including legal framework for stablecoin issuance, is expected to pass in the first quarter of 2026. The legislation would authorize KRW-backed stablecoins for the first time, ending nine years of prohibition on local cryptocurrency launches. *([koreatechdesk.com](https://koreatechdesk.com/korea-stablecoin-digital-asset-act-fintech-banks))*
- **Police investigate former presidential chief of staff in martial law probe** — Police questioned former presidential chief of staff Chung Jin-suk over his alleged role in former President Yoon Suk-yeol's brief martial law declaration in late 2024. Chung attended Cabinet meetings before and after the decree was imposed. *([koreatimes.co.kr](https://koreatimes.co.kr/southkorea/law-crime/20260208/police-question-ex-presidential-chief-of-staff-in-martial-law-probe))*
- **People Power Party plans name change and leadership restructuring** — The opposition People Power Party is targeting March 1 to unveil a new party name as part of broader reforms. The party is also centralizing nominations and replacing local chapter heads, including those from pro-Han factions opposing current leadership policies. *([biz.chosun.com](https://biz.chosun.com/en/en-policy/2026/02/07/66B3HIW3KVHZ5I2WHHPEEL6JHM/))*
